    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, whose authorship is debated in the Homeric Question, the Iliad was composed in the dactylic hexameter of Homeric Greek, probably in the late 8th century BCE, and runs to 24 books and 15,693 lines.</li><li>It covers weeks near the end of the ten-year siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>'s quarrel with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a> to his killing of Hector and the return of Hector's body to Priam; it does not depict the fall of Troy.</li><li>Recited by rhapsodes and taught in Greek schools, it shaped Greek education and ideas of war, and through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> versions, translations and retellings it has stayed central to European literature.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Contrary to popular belief, the Iliad neither depicts the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> nor mentions the Trojan Horse; both come from later sources.</p></aside>
    </section>

